dialogue joined_lantern
title: Off the lantern road
trigger: Joined lantern
speaker commander $commander
speaker hero $hero

node open commander
  The traveller from the Lantern Road has hung a small paper lantern on the wagon's hook and lit it, though the camp has fires enough.
  * You walked the road beside us for days before you joined. -> walked
    if: TrustAtLeast $hero 15
  * You've joined us. Sit a while. I'd like to know who rides with me. -> signed

node walked hero
  The road teaches patience. I wanted to see where you walked when no one was watching. You walked toward people, not away from them.
  -> lantern

node signed hero
  I came to the caravan because the road turned this way. On the Lantern Road that is reason enough.
  -> lantern

node lantern hero
  We keep the lanterns lit through the gorges, all the way to the pilgrim shrines. We do not ask why the Manalith hangs there. You ask a great deal, I think.
  -> asks

node asks commander
  The little lantern's flame leans toward the Commander, though there is no wind.
  * Somebody has to. The Choir counts on nobody asking. -> asking
    do: AddTrust $hero 6
  * I ask because I lost most of what I knew. I'm trying to find it again. -> lost
    do: AddTrust $hero 8
  * I ask the questions. You keep the light. That's how this works. -> roles
    do: AddTrust $hero -4

node asking hero
  The schools say a question is a blade without a guard. Still. I have carried this lantern for years and never once asked who it is lit for.
  -> master

node lost hero
  Lost. Then you are a pilgrim too, walking toward something you cannot see. The road is kind to those.
  -> master

node roles hero
  Simple roles. The sword-schools like them. I will keep the light, Commander. Mind that it does not show you only what you want to see.
  -> master

node master hero
  My master told me to walk the road and serve whoever needed the light. I did not expect it to lead to the Choir's tithe road.
  -> serve

node serve commander
  The traveller sets one hand flat on the wagon boards, the way the monks greet a shrine.
  * Then serve the ones on the road behind us. They need the light most. -> behind
    do: AddTrust $hero 8
  * Serve how you see fit. I won't tell you how to keep a lantern. -> free
    do: AddTrust $hero 6
  * Keep up and keep quiet. The road ahead is not for pilgrims. -> quiet
    do: AddTrust $hero -3

node behind hero
  Behind us. Yes. That is the oldest teaching of the road, and you found it without being told. I will keep it lit.

node free hero
  A Commander who leaves the lantern to its keeper. I will remember that when the road grows dark.

node quiet hero
  Quiet I can do. The road taught me that before it taught me anything else.
